MLOps Interview Series – Part 5

🔥 “Apache Airflow — The Most Important Tool Every MLOps Engineer Must Know”

If you're preparing for MLOps, Data Engineering, or ML Platform roles, you MUST understand Airflow.
 Here’s an interactive quiz-style breakdown that helps you learn exactly how Airflow works in real ML pipelines 👇

1️⃣ Why is Apache Airflow so popular?

Airflow gives you Python-based workflow orchestration with scheduling, retries, logs, monitoring, and dependency management — everything needed for reliable data & ML pipelines.

2️⃣ Where does Airflow fit in the ML lifecycle?

Airflow powers the automation layer of MLOps:
 ✔ Data ingestion
 ✔ Feature engineering
 ✔ Scheduled training
 ✔ Model evaluation
 ✔ Drift detection
 ✔ Retraining workflows

3️⃣ What is the input to an Airflow DAG?

Airflow does NOT ingest raw data.
 Its input is Python code that calls your scripts, SQL queries, APIs, containers, or Spark jobs.
 Airflow orchestrates — it doesn’t process.

4️⃣ What are the main functions of Airflow?

✔ Scheduling
 ✔ Orchestration
 ✔ Retries & error handling
 ✔ Logging & monitoring
 ✔ Backfills
 ✔ Alerts
 ✔ Dependency management
 ✔ UI visibility

5️⃣ What are the core components of Airflow?

DAGs – workflow definitions
Scheduler – decides what runs
Executor – runs tasks
Workers – execute workloads
Webserver UI – observability
Metastore DB – stores history

6️⃣ What is a DAG?

A Directed Acyclic Graph that defines task order and dependencies.
 It describes what runs and in what sequence — but not the actual processing.

7️⃣ What are Airflow Operators?

Templates that define task behavior:
 PythonOperator, BashOperator, DockerOperator, KubernetesPodOperator, SparkSubmitOperator, SQL operators, etc.

8️⃣ How does Airflow scale for large ML pipelines?

Airflow scales using different executors:
LocalExecutor → local parallelism
CeleryExecutor → distributed workers
KubernetesExecutor → each task runs in its own pod
 Perfect for data-heavy & ML-heavy workflows.

9️⃣ Common MLOps Use Cases

✔ Daily data ingestion
 ✔ Feature computation
 ✔ Model training jobs
 ✔ Batch inference
 ✔ Evaluation & drift detection
 ✔ Scheduled retraining
Airflow is the backbone of repeatable, production-grade ML.

🔟 What is Airflow NOT good at?
❌ Real-time inference
 ❌ Streaming pipelines
 ❌ Low-latency predictions
 ❌ Heavy GPU workloads inside tasks
Airflow = Batch Orchestration, not real-time.
